NAME
    hostname - the name of this machine

SYNOPSIS
    cat /etc/hostname

DESCRIPTION
    /etc/hostname holds the node name of the machine: one line, no
    spaces, as on any other Unix. It is the single source of truth, so
    everything that has to know who the machine is reads it from
    here instead of keeping a copy:

      - the vash prompt, which prints user@name:cwd$ where it used to
        print a hard-coded root@vnu:...
      - uname(2), so 'uname -n' and 'uname -a' agree with the prompt
      - the installer, which asks for the name and records it on the
        disk it writes (vnu install)

    A name is 1 to 63 characters of letters, digits, '-', '_' and
    '.', and must start and end with a letter or digit. 'vnu fetch'
    reports the current one as its Host line.

    The file is seeded at boot with the built-in default 'vnu'. An
    installed disk carries the name it was given, so booting that disk
    puts the name here instead. To rename a running system, write the
    file as root ('echo mybox > /etc/hostname') or call sethostname()
    from a program; the shell picks the new name up at the next
    prompt. Keep it short: it is part of every prompt.

    See 'man vnu' for the installer and 'man uname' for -n.

FILES
    /etc/hostname
